ention), makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation, professional communication). So strengths: reliability, stress, technical. Weaknesses: cooperation, communication, attention.\n\nCandidate C: makes correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated technology (technical), difficulty communicating ideas (communication), regarded as egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So strengths: decision, stress, technical. Weaknesses: communication, teamwork, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress/resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but regarded as know-it-all (cooperation), has hot temper (stress/cooperation). Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weaknesses: attitude, temper.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some issues. Which candidate has most balanced strengths? B has reliability and stress but poor communication. C has decision and stress but poor communication and egocentric. D has responsibility and problem solving but hot temper and know-it-all. A has technical and leadership but show-off and not open.\n\nProbably B seems strong on reliability and stress, which are critical, but nasty remarks are serious for crew cooperation. That may be disqualifying. D's hot temper also serious. C's egocentric also. A's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability.\n\nWe need to argue. Maybe we can argue that B's conscientiousness and stress handling outweigh communication issues, but we need to consider safety-critical. Nasty remarks could affect crew resource management. Might be a dealbreaker.\n\nMaybe D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but hot temper is dangerous under stress. Could be risk.\n\nC's quick correct decisions and calmness are good, but egocentric and communication difficulty could impair crew coordination.\n\nA's anticipation and leadership are valuable, but show-off and closed to ideas may affect teamwork.\n\nWhich candidate is best? Hard. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate B, despite communication issues, has the most reliable and stress-resilient profile, which are top criteria. We can propose to address communication via training. So recommendation B.\n\nWe need to reference discussion (none). We can say \"Given we are starting, I propose...\" That's okay.\n\nLet's produce public message stating recommendation Candidate B, referencing facts: very conscientious, handles stress well, but note communication issue. Explain why still best.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate B."}
